</style>

<main id="main-content" class="container py-5">
    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- Left Sidebar -->
        <div class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/HTML/Views/Shared/HTMLTopics.cshtml")
        </div>

        <!-- Main Content -->
        <div class="col-lg-9 content-section">

            <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">HTML Main Element</h1>

            <p>
                The HTML <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> element represents the primary content
                of a web page. It contains the content that is directly related to the
                main purpose of the document.
            </p>

            <div class="info-box">
                <strong>Key Point:</strong>
                The <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> element identifies the main content of the page.
                Repeated content such as major navigation, site headers, and footers
                generally belongs outside the main content area.
            </div>

            <h2>1. What is the main Element?</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> element is a semantic HTML element used
                to identify the dominant content of a document.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;main&gt;

    &lt;h1&gt;HTML Tutorial&lt;/h1&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;
        Learn HTML from basic to advanced concepts.
    &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;/main&gt;</code></pre>


<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
            <h3>Output</h3>

            <div class="demo-box">
                <main class="main-demo">
                    <h2 class="mt-0">HTML Tutorial</h2>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        Learn HTML from basic to advanced concepts.
                    </p>
                </main>
            </div>

            <h2>2. Purpose of the main Element</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> element identifies the central content
                of the document.
            </p>

            <p>
                This content can include:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Course content</li>
                <li>Blog articles</li>
                <li>Product information</li>
                <li>Documentation</li>
                <li>Search results</li>
                <li>Primary application content</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>3. Basic main Example</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;main&gt;

    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Website&lt;/h1&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;
        This is the main content of the page.
    &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;/main&gt;</code></pre>


<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
            <h2>4. main with Section</h2>

            <p>
                The main content can contain multiple thematic sections.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;main&gt;

    &lt;section&gt;

        &lt;h2&gt;HTML Basics&lt;/h2&gt;

        &lt;p&gt;
            Learn the fundamentals of HTML.
        &lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;/section&gt;

    &lt;section&gt;

        &lt;h2&gt;HTML Forms&lt;/h2&gt;

        &lt;p&gt;
            Learn how to create HTML forms.
        &lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;/section&gt;

&lt;/main&gt;</code></pre>


<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
            <h2>5. main with Article</h2>

            <p>
                Articles can be placed inside the main content area.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;main&gt;

    &lt;article&gt;

