            <h2>10. main vs div</h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-bordered align-middle">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Element</th>
                            <th>Purpose</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;main&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Represents the primary content of the page.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;div&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Generic container without a specific semantic meaning.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2>11. main vs section</h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-bordered align-middle">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Element</th>
                            <th>Purpose</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;main&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Identifies the primary content of the document.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>&lt;section&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Groups related content within a document.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2>12. main and Accessibility</h2>

            <p>
                Using the <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> element gives the document a clear
                semantic landmark for its primary content.
            </p>

            <p>
                This can help users of assistive technologies understand where the main
                content of the page begins.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;main&gt;

    &lt;h1&gt;HTML Tutorial&lt;/h1&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;
        Welcome to the HTML course.
    &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;/main&gt;</code></pre>


<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
